{
  "note": "Aphasias and Speaking Pathways",
  "source": "36ad0d20283c",
  "script": "1dfcaf097cad",
  "voice": "Patrick - Engaging and Warm",
  "model": "eleven_v4",
  "fingerprint": "0478392f28f9",
  "duration": 177.972,
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Aphasias and Speaking Pathways",
      "start": 0,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "The aphasias are language disorders, and they typically follow a stroke or a lesion in the relevant area.",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 6.08
        },
        {
          "text": "Those same areas also serve as the stations of the two speaking pathways.",
          "start": 6.32,
          "end": 11
        },
        {
          "text": "Whether you are repeating a word you've heard or reading a written word aloud, speech runs through these stations as an ordered sequence.",
          "start": 11.2,
          "end": 18.96
        },
        {
          "text": "That sequence ends at the motor cortex.",
          "start": 19.04,
          "end": 21.92
        },
        {
          "text": "We'll go through the three aphasias first, before following each of the two pathways in turn.",
          "start": 21.96,
          "end": 26.8
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Aphasias",
      "start": 27.638,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "First up is Broca's aphasia, which follows a lesion of Broca's area.",
          "start": 27.638,
          "end": 32.171
        },
        {
          "text": "It produces laboured, slow speech in a monotone, with impaired articulation.",
          "start": 32.225,
          "end": 37.291
        },
        {
          "text": "Comprehension, however, survives.",
          "start": 37.345,
          "end": 39.838
        },
        {
          "text": "Wernicke's aphasia follows a lesion of Wernicke's area, and it reverses that pattern.",
          "start": 39.878,
          "end": 44.718
        },
        {
          "text": "The speech is now fluent and melodic, yet it contains errors in word choice, and comprehension becomes difficult.",
          "start": 44.758,
          "end": 51.358
        },
        {
          "text": "Finally, there is conduction aphasia.",
          "start": 51.398,
          "end": 53.758
        },
        {
          "text": "This reflects damage to the connection between the two areas, rather than to either area itself.",
          "start": 53.798,
          "end": 59.118
        },
        {
          "text": "As a result, simple speech is intelligible and comprehension remains intact.",
          "start": 59.158,
          "end": 63.678
        },
        {
          "text": "Repeating sentences, on the other hand, is difficult.",
          "start": 63.718,
          "end": 66.798
        },
        {
          "text": "That's because repetition must be relayed directly from Wernicke's area to Broca's area.",
          "start": 66.838,
          "end": 72.038
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Speaking a heard word",
      "start": 72.803,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Now let's follow the route for speaking a word that has been heard.",
          "start": 72.803,
          "end": 76.376
        },
        {
          "text": "It starts at the auditory cortex, which receives the sound of the word.",
          "start": 76.43,
          "end": 80.59
        },
        {
          "text": "The word is then passed to Wernicke's area, where it is understood.",
          "start": 80.616,
          "end": 84.363
        },
        {
          "text": "From there it is relayed to Broca's area, which plans the utterance.",
          "start": 84.403,
          "end": 88.683
        },
        {
          "text": "Lastly, it is delivered to the motor cortex for execution.",
          "start": 88.723,
          "end": 92.536
        },
        {
          "text": "Put simply, the route runs from the auditory cortex to Wernicke's area, on to Broca's area, and ends at the motor cortex.",
          "start": 92.59,
          "end": 100.483
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Speaking a written word",
      "start": 101.251,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "What about a written word?",
          "start": 101.251,
          "end": 103.091
        },
        {
          "text": "Reading aloud adds two steps at the front of that same route.",
          "start": 103.171,
          "end": 106.971
        },
        {
          "text": "This time, the written word is received by the visual cortex.",
          "start": 107.011,
          "end": 110.811
        },
        {
          "text": "Next, the angular gyrus converts it into its spoken form.",
          "start": 110.851,
          "end": 114.744
        },
        {
          "text": "From that point on, it follows exactly the same sequence as a heard word, through Wernicke's area and Broca's area to the motor cortex.",
          "start": 114.798,
          "end": 122.798
        },
        {
          "text": "So the full route runs from the visual cortex to the angular gyrus, and from there to Wernicke's area, Broca's area and the motor cortex.",
          "start": 122.824,
          "end": 131.131
        },
        {
          "text": "Notice that the auditory cortex isn't needed in this case.",
          "start": 131.171,
          "end": 134.931
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Summary",
      "start": 135.706,
      "sentences": [
        {
          "text": "Let's sum up.",
          "start": 135.706,
          "end": 136.906
        },
        {
          "text": "Aphasias are language disorders that follow a stroke or lesion of the relevant area.",
          "start": 136.986,
          "end": 142.186
        },
        {
          "text": "Broca's aphasia gives laboured, slow speech in a monotone.",
          "start": 142.266,
          "end": 146.066
        },
        {
          "text": "Wernicke's aphasia gives fluent, melodic speech, but with errors in word choice and poor comprehension.",
          "start": 146.106,
          "end": 152.159
        },
        {
          "text": "Conduction aphasia, meanwhile, impairs repetition alone.",
          "start": 152.213,
          "end": 155.986
        },
        {
          "text": "As for the pathways, speaking a heard word runs from the auditory cortex to Wernicke's area, then Broca's area, and finally the motor cortex.",
          "start": 156.026,
          "end": 165.026
        },
        {
          "text": "Speaking a written word begins instead at the visual cortex and passes through the angular gyrus, before joining that same route.",
          "start": 165.066,
          "end": 172.719
        },
        {
          "text": "And that completes the aphasias, along with the two routes that speech takes to the motor cortex.",
          "start": 172.773,
          "end": 178.026
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
