    {"duration":42.266122,"alignment":{"characters":"Let's sum up. Aphasias are language disorders that follow a stroke or lesion of the relevant area. Broca's aphasia gives laboured, slow speech in a monotone. Wernicke's aphasia gives fluent, melodic speech, but with errors in word choice and poor comprehension. Conduction aphasia, meanwhile, impairs repetition alone. As for the pathways, speaking a heard word runs from the auditory cortex to Wernicke's area, then Broca's area, and finally the motor cortex. Speaking a written word begins instead at the visual cortex and passes through the angular gyrus, before joining that same route. And that completes the aphasias, along with the two routes that speech takes to the motor cortex.","starts":[0,53,107,160,200,240,320,373,427,480,560,640,720,1200,1280,1360,1420,1480,1540,1600,1707,1813,1920,2000,2027,2053,2080,2160,2200,2240,2280,2320,2360,2400,2440,2480,2560,2640,2720,2800,2853,2907,2960,3013,3067,3120,3200,3220,3240,3260,3280,3360,3400,3440,3480,3520,3560,3600,3640,3680,3760,3827,3893,3960,4027,4093,4160,4240,4280,4320,4400,4453,4507,4560,4640,4720,4800,4880,4920,4960,5000,5013,5027,5040,5120,5170,5220,5270,5320,5370,5420,5470,5520,5600,5800,6000,6200,6400,6480,6560,6587,6613,6640,6680,6720,6760,6800,6920,6960,7000,7040,7120,7200,7280,7360,7440,7520,7540,7560,7580,7600,7680,7760,7840,7920,7984,8048,8112,8176,8240,8280,8320,8400,8480,8560,8640,8720,8773,8827,8880,8933,8987,9040,9120,9160,9200,9240,9280,9360,9413,9467,9520,9640,9760,9947,10133,10320,10360,10400,10480,10507,10533,10560,10600,10640,10680,10720,10760,10800,10920,10960,11000,11040,11120,11200,11280,11360,11400,11440,11480,11520,11560,11600,11680,11720,11760,11840,11920,12000,12080,12120,12160,12240,12320,12400,12460,12520,12580,12640,12720,12800,12880,12960,13040,13120,13200,13227,13253,13262,13271,13280,13360,13400,13440,13480,13520,13600,13640,13680,13720,13760,13800,13840,13920,13960,14000,14080,14140,14200,14260,14320,14520,14553,14587,14620,14653,14